From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:42:52 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] internal/poll: post AcceptEx from a thread that never blocks in synchronous I/O

Windows 8 and Server 2012, kernel 6.2, deliver the completion of an
overlapped AcceptEx only once the thread that posted it is out of any
synchronous I/O call (Microsoft Connect #760161). Windows 7 and Windows 8.1,
kernels 6.1 and 6.3, deliver it at once. AcceptEx has always been posted by
whichever thread runs the accepting goroutine, and os.Pipe has always been a
synchronous pipe, so os/exec and testing's example capture can hold a
listener's accept while they read a child.

No upstream change introduced this. CL 457695 (Go 1.20) made that the last
release for Windows 8, and upstream's builders ran Server 2008 R2 and 2012 R2,
kernels 6.1 and 6.3, so no Go test ever ran on kernel 6.2.

	thread posts AcceptEx  ->  the same thread reads a child's pipe
	connection arrives     ->  Win7, Win8.1+  completion delivered
	                           Win8, 6.2      completion held for the read
	child waits on server  ->  Win8, 6.2      the read never returns

On kernel 6.2 every listener in the process stops accepting, which is what
stopped cmd/go's test proxy and testing's example capture.

Post every AcceptEx on kernel 6.2 from the one thread of a private thread
pool, which is never in such a call. Every other kernel posts as it did, and
the pipes stay synchronous.

